{
  "summary": "Critiqued the hybrid approach on four key weaknesses: (1) Mechanical raw extraction of plan summaries, review feedback, and impl_notes produces noise rather than signal — these fields were written for their own pipeline stages, not as cross-feature context, so dumping them verbatim into BOARD_CONTEXT.md creates a bloated file full of feature-specific implementation details that waste context window and confuse future agents. (2) The done_script mechanism is the wrong integration point — done_script is currently a per-feature, user-configured single string reference; hijacking it for a built-in system behavior changes its semantics from 'optional user hook' to 'system behavior plus optional user hook,' creating a leaky abstraction. The cleaner integration is a post-transition hook in move_to_stage() in state.py, which is the single canonical transition point and already called by both TUI paths and the server. (3) The 'human curation' strategy is unrealistic as a quality gate — if the file fills with raw mechanical extractions, curating it becomes a tedious chore that users will ignore, and then every agent on the board gets a 30KB blob of mostly irrelevant past details prepended to every prompt, degrading rather than improving agent performance. The feature's entire value proposition is reducing manual effort, so punting the hardest problem (quality) to the user undermines the design. (4) No consideration of selective context inclusion — the proposal prepends BOARD_CONTEXT.md to every agent prompt for every pipeline stage, but a planning agent doesn't need the same cross-feature context as a review agent. A better approach would tag entries by relevance category (architectural decisions vs. code patterns vs. gotchas) and let each pipeline stage pull only what's relevant, or at minimum only include board context for planning and implementation stages where cross-feature awareness actually matters."
}
